1. 

The background is that there is a machine with an Allen-Bradley PLC (Micro850), which needs to interact with an industrial server on which runs my python program.

The machine has 4 stations. Station 1 is where one does a few manual steps. Station 2 is for tightening a nut. Station 3 is for checking the torque required for free rotation.
Station 4 is for a cap pressing operation. The machine noves back and forth between station 2 and 3 a couple of times.

2.

I want a python class called IOServer that can be called from another module as IOServer(). IOServer helps me communicate with the PLC. 

The machine is question goes through a set of states. The various states defined in MachineState.py give you an idea of what states the machine will be in.

For some states, I need to read a tag from the PLC, then do some action (like taking a picture and analysing it, etc), then write a tag back to the PLC, and then change the state of the machine to the next state, if the write succeeds. 

In some other states, I just need to read some tag from the PLC and change the state in the IOServer to the next state e.g. If I need to read a torque value from the PLC, then, after the value stabilises (which means I might need to read it 2-3 times. The number of times that I read it, is defined as a class variable), then I should change machineState in IOServer to the next state.

For each state, I have a specific variable defined in the PLC that I need to track. The idea is to run a read thread in the IOServer that sleeps for a class-defined period of time (e.g. 100 msecs), then wakes up, and reads specific tags from the PLC, depending on which state the machine is in. For some states, it reads only 1 tag. For some others, it reads 2. Which tag (or tags) to read in each state is known, and which tag or tags to write is also known, as per the details below.

